{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1415580-homebrew-thoughts-porting-some-scion-concepts-to-mage","id":1415580,"title":"Homebrew thoughts: Porting some Scion concepts to Mage","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-09-26T11:52:23-07:00","id":1415580,"title":"Homebrew thoughts: Porting some Scion concepts to Mage","content":"Apparently I already had an account on this forum. Who knew?<br /> <br /> Mage was always my first love in gaming, but I haven\u2019t played it in a while. Recently, I\u2019ve been running a Scion 2e game, and there are a couple of mechanics from the Story Path system that I\u2019ve been thinking would fit well with Mage.<br /> <br /> First, I\u2019ve been thinking about the paths themselves, particularly at character creation. I like how the paths give players a chance to think about where their characters come from, who they are now, and how they fit into their society. The origin and role paths help ground characters in their histories and communities, which I really like. While Vampire is all about gradually losing your ties to your humanity, Magi at least can remain connected to their cultures and communities, and often those things are part of where they get their power. If you used to the last path to represent both Tradition/affiliation and paradigm, it would give a more specific space to think about what abilities a character might need to function within their paradigm as well as to think about how there character\u2019s fit into their group as an institution of it\u2019s own and not just as a category to put their character in. I also like that this could take some pressure off of backgrounds: a character can know some people, have access to some resources, etc. without spending all of their background points and can use backgrounds for things that are truly exceptional about their character. If my character\u2019s role is \u201csoccer mom,\u201d I have a minivan and a suburban house, and know the soccer coach and the PTA without having to spend the points I want to use to make sure that the Etherite laboratory in the basement counts as my sanctum. I can call my friend on the PTA or use some of my family\u2019s income once a game, or twice if I\u2019m willing to risk some pull coming from the other end, and I could still use the resources background if I wanted to say that I had some extra money I had squirrelled away that my husband doesn\u2019t know about to fund my projects, but I don\u2019t need to put points there just to make my concept work.<br /> <br /> The other part that intrigued me was the Crafting rules, although not necessarily to be used for crafting things. I actually feel like a system like this would be great fit for \u201critual\u201d casting. Basically what I\u2019m suggesting is that quick casting would remain simply a straight Arete role, but that ritual casting would instead involve a series of different attribute + ability roles, with the number of roles maxing out at your arete. In Scion, each role allows you to overcome a specific barrier to crafting, but I envision this primarily as simply accumulating successes.<br /> <br /> Right now, ritual casting ends up being a long series of relatively small roles. It often feels tedious and doesn\u2019t really tie in to the rest of the character sheet that well. Under current rules, a normal starting character, with 3 Arete and 5 Willpower could role their arete up to 8 times for a total of 24 dice. This system might keep the number of actual dice rolled (and therefor the number of accumulated successes) a little lower: the same character with 3 roles, with varying attributes + abilities, would probably translate to between 15 and 20 dice total depending. But it would resolve much faster in terms of play, and I think it might be more narratively satisfying. Instead of describing a ritual that is relatively divorced from the mechanics, and then hunkering down for a long series of dice roles, the player describes a smaller series of actions that connect directly to the mechanics. So instead of \u201cI research the correct sigil and then carefully draw it out while chanting the secret names,\u201d it could be \u201cI do a perception + research role to find the correct sigil, and then use my dexterity + craft ability to carefully write it out, and then a charisma + esoterica role to pronounce the words correctly.\u201d I feel like this would also encourage players to pursue mundane abilities that tie into their character's paradigm.<br /> <br /> So, thoughts about these as house rules? Things you'd change before trying to implement? Are there other things you would consider porting from Scion or StoryPath?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24383-laughinggull","name":"LaughingGull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24383_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-27T15:31:56-07:00","id":1415750,"title":"","content":"My primary thoughts are:<br /> <br /> 1) Start with Trinity 2e over Scion 2e. Trinity is built closer to the WoD in a few ways (Edges are closer to Backgrounds than Birthrights are, and Edges are included with Paths on top of Connections), and has better support for a spectrum of things between mundane and fantastical (esp. for running technomages).<br /> <br /> 2) It's probably easier to port the Mage magic system into Storypath, than try to make Paths work in the WoD system.<br /> <br /> The whole Milestone take on extended actions by itself isn't a bad house rule for the WoD over the default &quot;roll the same thing over and over&quot; approach. That said, for magical casting, I'm not sure how great of a fit it is.<br /> <br /> What I would do is making the performance of the ritual a Milestone based action, with the number of Milestones and types of them translating into more concrete bonuses for the final result, and then end with a normal casting roll. The end result would largely look the same, but with less huge numbers of successes to then allocate in a fashion that doesn't necessarily connect to the ritual. So making a perfect summoning circle isn't about racking up extra successes on the magic roll, but it lowers the effective Gauntlet rating and improves the summoned entity's disposition towards the mage based on how many Milestones and how well those rolls went.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}